`,s.jsxs(e.li,{children:[s.jsx(e.strong,{children:"FPR cap at 0.3"}),": The metric only considers thresholds up to a 30% false positive rate. This encourages conservative predictions — predicting everything as anomalous would yield perfect recall but at FPR=1.0, which contributes nothing to the score."]}),`
`]}),`
`,s.jsx(e.p,{children:"This metric explains several design choices:"}),`
`,s.jsxs(e.ul,{children:[`
`,s.jsx(e.li,{children:"Global smoothing helps ensure that even small defect regions produce a contiguous high-score region (preventing them from being missed at any threshold)."}),`
`,s.jsx(e.li,{children:"The per-region focus means that a single missed defect disproportionately hurts, so it is better to slightly over-predict (at the cost of some FPR) than to miss a defect entirely."}),`
`]}),`
`,s.jsx(e.h2,{id:"discussion-why-this-approach-works",children:s.jsx(e.a,{href:"#discussion-why-this-approach-works",children:"Discussion: Why This Approach Works"})}),`
`,s.jsx(e.p,{children:"The solution's elegance lies in its simplicity. There is no complex training loop, no iterative optimization, no data augmentation. The three-step pipeline — feature extraction, mean subtraction, and post-processing — achieves a Leaderboard B score of 0.7492 AUPRO."}),`
`,s.jsx(e.p,{children:"Why does such a simple approach work so well?"}),`
`,s.jsxs(e.ol,{children:[`
`,s.jsxs(e.li,{children:[`
`,s.jsxs(e.p,{children:[s.jsx(e.strong,{children:"Feature space separation"}),": ImageNet-pretrained features, even without fine-tuning, naturally separate anomalous patches from normal ones in Euclidean space. This is because the features encode texture, edge density, and color distribution — precisely the attributes that distinguish defects from normal surface."]}),`
`]}),`
`,s.jsxs(e.li,{children:[`
`,s.jsxs(e.p,{children:[s.jsx(e.strong,{children:"The mean is surprisingly robust"}),": Despite averaging over all spatial locations, the global mean feature vector is dominated by normal hazelnut surface texture. Defects affect at most ~0.23–28% of pixels in test images, and the training set contains zero defects, so the centroid cleanly represents normality."]}),`
`]}),`
`,s.jsxs(e.li,{children:[`
`,s.jsxs(e.p,{children:[s.jsx(e.strong,{children:"Multi-scale feature encoding"}),": ResNet18's layer2 features have a receptive field of approximately 35×35 pixels in the input (after successive convolutions and pooling). This means each feature vector encodes a neighborhood larger than a single 16×16 patch, allowing the model to detect defects that alter the local texture pattern even if the exact defect pixel is not perfectly aligned with the feature grid."]}),`
`]}),`
`]}),`
`,s.jsx(e.h2,{id:"potential-improvements",children:s.jsx(e.a,{href:"#potential-improvements",children:"Potential Improvements"})}),`
`,s.jsx(e.p,{children:"While the baseline is strong, several directions could yield further gains:"}),`
`,s.jsxs(e.ul,{children:[`
`,s.jsxs(e.li,{children:[s.jsx(e.strong,{children:"Per-location mean"}),": Instead of a global mean, compute a separate mean for each of the 64×64 spatial positions. This accounts for the fact that the hazelnut edge, the background, and the nut center have different normal appearances."]}),`
`,s.jsxs(e.li,{children:[s.jsx(e.strong,{children:"Multi-scale features"}),": Concatenate features from multiple ResNet layers (e.g., layer1 and layer2) to capture both fine texture and mid-level structure."]}),`
`,s.jsxs(e.li,{children:[s.jsx(e.strong,{children:"Soft histogram or KDE modeling"}),": Instead of L2 distance to the mean, model the full feature distribution with a kernel density estimate or a small Gaussian mixture model. This can capture multi-modal feature distributions (e.g., the nut surface may have several distinct normal textures)."]}),`
`,s.jsxs(e.li,{children:[s.jsx(e.strong,{children:"Test-time augmentation"}),": Average anomaly scores across multiple augmented views (flips, rotations) of each test image to reduce variance."]}),`
`,s.jsxs(e.li,{children:[s.jsx(e.strong,{children:"Learnable thresholding"}),": Optimize the global normalization strategy by learning the quantile clipping points on a validation split."]}),`
`]}),`
